### What is a HashMap?

A HashMap is a data structure that stores data as key-value pairs and lets you retrieve a value by its key in average constant time. Internally it keeps an array of buckets. When you call `put(key, value)`, the map computes `key.hashCode()`, applies a bit-spreading function, and uses the result to pick a bucket index. The entry is stored in that bucket. When you call `get(key)`, the same hash computation finds the bucket, and the map walks the bucket's chain comparing keys with `equals()` to find the matching entry.

```java
package com.bank.core;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.HashMap;
import java.util.Map;

public class AccountBalanceStore {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Map<String, BigDecimal> balances = new HashMap<>();
        balances.put("SAV2001", new BigDecimal("1000000.00"));
        balances.put("CUR3001", new BigDecimal("500000.00"));

        BigDecimal rajesh = balances.get("SAV2001");
        System.out.println("Rajesh balance: " + rajesh);
    }
}
```

```text
Rajesh balance: 1000000.00
```

### How does the hash function work internally?

Java's HashMap does not use `hashCode()` directly. It applies a secondary hash that XORs the upper 16 bits of the hash code with the lower 16 bits. This spreads the information across all bits and reduces collisions when the bucket array is small. The bucket index is then computed as `(n - 1) & hash`, where n is the array length and is always a power of two, so the bitwise AND acts as a fast modulo.

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class HashDemo {
    static int spread(int h) {
        return h ^ (h >>> 16);
    }

    static int bucketIndex(int hash, int capacity) {
        return (capacity - 1) & hash;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String key = "SAV2001";
        int raw = key.hashCode();
        int spread = spread(raw);
        int index = bucketIndex(spread, 16);
        System.out.println("Raw hash: " + raw);
        System.out.println("Spread hash: " + spread);
        System.out.println("Bucket index (cap 16): " + index);
    }
}
```

### How does collision handling work?

When two keys hash to the same bucket, a collision occurs. Java chains the entries in that bucket as a linked list. Each node holds the key, value, hash, and a pointer to the next node. On lookup the map walks this chain and compares each key using `equals()`. Since Java 8, once a bucket holds eight or more entries and the table has at least 64 buckets, the linked list converts into a red-black tree, which drops the worst-case lookup from O(n) to O(log n).

```java
package com.bank.core;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.HashMap;
import java.util.Map;

public class CollisionDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // These two accounts may land in the same bucket
        Map<String, BigDecimal> balances = new HashMap<>(4);
        balances.put("SAV2001", new BigDecimal("100000"));
        balances.put("SAV2005", new BigDecimal("200000"));
        balances.put("CUR3001", new BigDecimal("300000"));
        // If SAV2001 and CUR3001 collide, they chain in the same bucket
        System.out.println("Lookup SAV2001: " + balances.get("SAV2001"));
        System.out.println("Lookup CUR3001: " + balances.get("CUR3001"));
    }
}
```

```text
Lookup SAV2001: 100000
Lookup CUR3001: 300000
```

### How does resizing work?

The map tracks a load factor, which defaults to 0.75. When the number of entries exceeds the load factor times the current capacity, the map doubles the internal array and rehashes every entry into the new, larger array. Doubling keeps the bucket count as a power of two, which is required for the bitwise index computation. Pre-sizing the map avoids unnecessary resizes.

```java
package com.bank.core;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.HashMap;
import java.util.Map;

public class PreSizedMap {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int expectedAccounts = 10000;
        // Pre-size to avoid resizing: capacity = expected / loadFactor + 1
        Map<String, BigDecimal> balances = new HashMap<>(
            (int) (expectedAccounts / 0.75) + 1
        );
        for (int i = 0; i < expectedAccounts; i++) {
            balances.put("ACC-" + i, BigDecimal.valueOf(i * 1000L));
        }
        System.out.println("Stored " + balances.size() + " accounts without resizing.");
    }
}
```

### What is the equals and hashCode contract?

If two objects are equal according to `equals()`, they must return the same `hashCode()`. Breaking this contract causes the map to store duplicates or fail to find existing keys. If you override `equals()` on a custom key class, you must also override `hashCode()` to be consistent.

```java
package com.bank.model;

import java.util.Objects;

public class AccountKey {
    private final String branchCode;
    private final String accountNumber;

    public AccountKey(String branchCode, String accountNumber) {
        this.branchCode = branchCode;
        this.accountNumber = accountNumber;
    }

    @Override
    public boolean equals(Object o) {
        if (this == o) return true;
        if (!(o instanceof AccountKey)) return false;
        AccountKey that = (AccountKey) o;
        return branchCode.equals(that.branchCode)
            && accountNumber.equals(that.accountNumber);
    }

    @Override
    public int hashCode() {
        return Objects.hash(branchCode, accountNumber);
    }
}
```

### What is the BigDecimal equals trap?

BigDecimal's `equals()` method considers scale, so `new BigDecimal("1.0")` and `new BigDecimal("1.00")` are not equal and may hash differently. This means a HashMap keyed on BigDecimal can store what look like duplicate amounts. To avoid this, either normalise the scale with `stripTrailingZeros()` before insertion, or use a `TreeMap` with `BigDecimal::compareTo` as the comparator.

```java
package com.bank.core;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.HashMap;
import java.util.Map;

public class BigDecimalTrap {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Map<BigDecimal, String> map = new HashMap<>();
        map.put(new BigDecimal("1.0"), "first");
        map.put(new BigDecimal("1.00"), "second");
        // Two entries because equals considers scale
        System.out.println("Size: " + map.size()); // 2, not 1

        // Fix: normalise with stripTrailingZeros
        Map<BigDecimal, String> fixed = new HashMap<>();
        fixed.put(new BigDecimal("1.0").stripTrailingZeros(), "first");
        fixed.put(new BigDecimal("1.00").stripTrailingZeros(), "second");
        System.out.println("Fixed size: " + fixed.size()); // 1
    }
}
```

```text
Size: 2
Fixed size: 1
```

### What about thread safety?

A plain HashMap is not thread-safe. If two threads call `put()` concurrently, the internal structure can corrupt, leading to lost entries or infinite loops during resize. In a multi-threaded banking service, use `ConcurrentHashMap`, which partitions the buckets into segments and allows concurrent reads and writes without locking the entire map.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.concurrent.ConcurrentHashMap;
import java.util.concurrent.ConcurrentMap;

public class ConcurrentBalanceStore {
    private final ConcurrentMap<String, BigDecimal> balances =
        new ConcurrentHashMap<>();

    public void updateBalance(String accountId, BigDecimal newBalance) {
        balances.put(accountId, newBalance);
    }

    public BigDecimal getBalance(String accountId) {
        return balances.getOrDefault(accountId, BigDecimal.ZERO);
    }
}
```

### What are common mistakes?

The most common mistake is using a mutable object as a key and then modifying it after insertion. Changing the object changes its hash code, and the map can no longer find it in the correct bucket, making the entry unreachable.

```java
// Wrong: mutable key
List<String> key = new ArrayList<>(List.of("SAV", "2001"));
Map<List<String>, String> map = new HashMap<>();
map.put(key, "Rajesh");
key.add("EXTRA"); // hash changes
System.out.println(map.get(key)); // null, entry is lost

// Correct: use an immutable key
String safeKey = "SAV-2001";
Map<String, String> safeMap = new HashMap<>();
safeMap.put(safeKey, "Rajesh");
System.out.println(safeMap.get(safeKey)); // Rajesh
```

### How is HashMap different from TreeMap and LinkedHashMap?

| Feature | HashMap | TreeMap | LinkedHashMap |
|---|---|---|---|
| Ordering | None | Sorted by key | Insertion order |
| Lookup time | O(1) average | O(log n) | O(1) average |
| Null keys | One allowed | Not allowed | One allowed |
| Use case | General fast lookup | Range queries | LRU cache, ordered iteration |

```java
package com.bank.core;

import java.util.*;

public class MapComparison {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Map<String, Integer> hashMap = new HashMap<>();
        Map<String, Integer> treeMap = new TreeMap<>();
        Map<String, Integer> linkedMap = new LinkedHashMap<>();

        for (String key : List.of("CUR", "SAV", "FDR", "LON")) {
            hashMap.put(key, 1);
            treeMap.put(key, 1);
            linkedMap.put(key, 1);
        }
        System.out.println("HashMap order:       " + hashMap.keySet());
        System.out.println("TreeMap order:        " + treeMap.keySet());
        System.out.println("LinkedHashMap order:  " + linkedMap.keySet());
    }
}
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

A senior engineer knows the internal mechanics: the bit-spreading secondary hash, the power-of-two capacity, the linked-list-to-tree threshold, and the resize trigger. They pre-size maps to avoid unnecessary resizes in hot paths, use immutable keys, and choose ConcurrentHashMap for concurrent access. They also know that Java 8 introduced the `compute`, `merge`, and `computeIfAbsent` methods, which simplify patterns like counting frequencies or building adjacency lists.
